 359	\begin{definition}[The combining map on polynomial tuples]
 360		\label{def:ld-combining-map}
 361		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.combinePolyTuple, MIPStarRE.QPBT.combinePolyTuple_eval}
 362		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.combinePolyTuple_mem_polyFunc}
 363		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedPolynomial}
 364		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedPolynomial_eval}
 365		\leanok
 366		\uses{def:polyfunc, def:ld-meas, def:ld-combining-parameters}
 367		Coordinates of $\F_q^{m+k}$ are written $(u,\alpha)$, with $u \in \F_q^m$
 368		occupying the first $m$ coordinates and $\alpha \in \F_q^k$ the last $k$;
 369		the coordinates carrying $\alpha$ are called the \emph{combining
 370		coordinates}.  For a tuple $g = (g_0,\ldots,g_{k-1})$ of polynomials
 371		$g_r \in \polyfunc{m}{q}{d}$, the \emph{combined polynomial} is
 372		\[
 373			\mathrm{comb}_g(u,\alpha) \;=\; \sum_{r=0}^{k-1} \alpha_r\, g_r(u)\;.
 374		\]
 375		It lies in $\polyfunc{m+k}{q}{d}$ whenever $d \ge 1$: a component $g_r$
 376		involves no combining coordinate, so multiplying it by $\alpha_r$ leaves its
 377		individual degrees in the first $m$ coordinates unchanged and produces
 378		individual degree $1$ in the coordinate carrying $\alpha_r$.
 379	
 380		The order of the two coordinate blocks is part of the construction.  The
 381		point coordinates precede the combining coordinates, so that zeroing a
 382		prefix of a direction of $\F_q^{m+k}$, as the diagonal-line branch of
 383		Definition~\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} does, restricts on the first
 384		$m$ coordinates to zeroing the corresponding prefix of the point part of
 385		that direction; this is what makes the diagonal-line questions of the
 386		combined game project to diagonal-line questions of the original game in
 387		Definition~\ref{def:ld-combined-strategy}.  Definition~\ref{def:combine-map}
 388		is a variant of the same construction with two components in \emph{disjoint}
 389		blocks of point coordinates; the map above keeps one common block of point
 390		coordinates, as the reduction requires.
 391	\end{definition}
