 452	\begin{definition}[The combined strategy]
 453		\label{def:ld-combined-strategy}
 454		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedMeasuredQuestion}
 455		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedMeasuredDirection}
 456		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedRebaseParameter}
 457		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedAnswerMap}
 458		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedStrategy}
 459		\leanok
 460		\uses{def:ld-game, def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate,
 461			def:tensor-product-strategy, def:bracket,
 462			def:ld-combining-parameters, def:ld-combining-map}
 463		Let $\mathcal S = (\psi, A, B)$ be a strategy for the directly indexed
 464		low-degree game with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$.  The
 465		\emph{combined strategy} $\mathcal S^{\mathrm c}$ for the directly indexed
 466		low-degree game with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}^{\mathrm c}$ has the same
 467		state and the same two Hilbert spaces, and answers each question of the
 468		combined game by measuring one question of $\mathcal S$ and post-processing
 469		its outcome.  Writing questions of the combined game in the coordinates of
 470		Definition~\ref{def:ld-combining-map}, the five cases are the following.
 471		\begin{enumerate}
 472			\item On the point question $(u,\alpha) \in \F_q^{m+k}$: measure the
 473			point question $u$, obtaining $b \in \F_q^k$, and answer
 474			$\sum_r \alpha_r b_r$.
 475			\item On the axis-parallel line question with stored index $i < m$ and
 476			canonical base $(u_0,\alpha_0)$: measure the axis-parallel line question
 477			with index $i$ and base $u_0$, obtaining univariate polynomials
 478			$f_0,\ldots,f_{k-1}$ of degree at most $d$, and answer
 479			$\sum_r (\alpha_0)_r f_r$, again of degree at most $d$.
 480			\item On the axis-parallel line question with stored index $m + r$ and
 481			canonical base $(u_0,\alpha_0)$, a line along which $u$ is constant and
 482			only the coordinate $\alpha_r$ varies: measure the point question $u_0$,
 483			obtaining $b$, and answer the degree-one polynomial
 484			$t \mapsto \sum_s (\alpha_0)_s b_s + t\, b_r$.
 485			\item On the diagonal line question with stored index $i < m$, canonical
 486			base $(u_0,\alpha_0)$ and direction $(v,w)$: measure the diagonal line
 487			question with index $i$, direction $v$ and the canonical base of the
 488			line $\ell(u_0, v)$, rebase the answer along the affine change of
 489			parameter relating the two presentations of that line, obtaining
 490			univariate polynomials $f_0,\ldots,f_{k-1}$ of degree at most $md$ in the
 491			parameter of the combined line, and answer
 492			$t \mapsto \sum_r \bigl((\alpha_0)_r + t\, w_r\bigr) f_r(t)$, of degree
 493			at most $md+1 \le (m+k)d$.
 494			\item On the diagonal line question with stored index $i \ge m$,
 495			canonical base $(u_0,\alpha_0)$ and direction $(0,w)$, a line along which
 496			$u$ is constant: measure the point question $u_0$, obtaining $b$, and
 497			answer $t \mapsto \sum_r (\alpha_0)_r b_r + t \sum_r w_r b_r$.
 498		\end{enumerate}
 499		For any of these five question forms, if the outcome of the measured
 500		question has a different answer form from the one specified in the
 501		corresponding case, the post-processing returns the zero answer of the form
 502		required by the combined question.  This convention makes the
 503		post-processing a total map on the common answer alphabet.
 504		For the point measurements of the original strategy, let $r$ be the map
 505		that sends a point answer to its tuple in $\F_q^k$ and sends either kind of
 506		line answer to the zero tuple.  Write
 507		\[
 508			\widehat A^{\mathsf{Point},u}_a
 509			= \sum_{x:\,r(x)=a} A^{\mathsf{Point},u}_x,
 510			\qquad
 511			\widehat B^{\mathsf{Point},u}_a
 512			= \sum_{x:\,r(x)=a} B^{\mathsf{Point},u}_x.
 513		\]
 514		These are the point measurements post-processed by
 515		\texttt{directLdPointValuesOrZero}.  In particular, each zero-tuple effect
 516		is the original zero-tuple point effect plus all wrong-form answer effects;
 517		those additional effects need not vanish.  Use the same convention for the
 518		combined strategy, with its own answer alphabet.
 519	
 520		Cases~3 and~5 use that a line of $\F_q^{m+k}$ whose direction
 521		has vanishing point part is contained in a fiber of the projection to
 522		$\F_q^m$, so that the combined polynomial restricted to it is determined by
 523		the $k$ values of the tuple at the single point $u_0$; the rebasing in
 524		case~4 is the affine change of parameter of the previous paragraph, needed
 525		because the
 526		canonical base of a line of $\F_q^{m+k}$ need not project to the canonical
 527		base of its image line in $\F_q^m$.
 528	\end{definition}
