 595	\begin{lemma}[Value of the combined strategy]
 596		\label{lem:ld-combined-value}
 597		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directLdRejectionProbability_directCombinedStrategy_le}
 598		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedStrategy_value_ge}
 599		\leanok
 600		\uses{def:ld-combined-strategy, lem:ld-combined-question-law,
 601			def:ld-win-predicate, def:tensor-product-value}
 602		The statement holds with the universal constant $C = 10$: if $\mathcal S$
 603		succeeds with probability at least $1 - \eps$ in the directly indexed
 604		low-degree game with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$, then
 605		$\mathcal S^{\mathrm c}$ succeeds with probability at least $1 - 10\eps$ in
 606		the directly indexed low-degree game with parameters
 607		$\mathsf{ldparams}^{\mathrm c}$.  The value $10$ is used in the absorption of
 608		Proposition~\ref{prop:ld-simultaneous-general-k}.
 609	\end{lemma}
 610	\begin{proof}
 611		\leanok
 612		\uses{def:ld-combined-strategy, lem:ld-combined-question-law,
 613			def:ld-win-predicate}
 614		Both games weight their nine ordered type pairs uniformly, so it suffices to
 615		bound the rejection probability of each branch of the combined game by a
 616		bounded number of branch rejection probabilities of the original game and to
 617		sum.  Fix a branch and split according to the event of
 618		Lemma~\ref{lem:ld-combined-question-law}.
 619	
 620		On the event $\mathbf{i} < m$ the two combined answers are the images of the
 621		two original answers under the relabelings of
 622		Definition~\ref{def:ld-combined-strategy}, and each clause of
 623		Definition~\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} for the combined game follows from the
 624		corresponding clause for the original game.  For the point-versus-point
 625		clause this is immediate.  For a line-versus-point clause, let $t$ present
 626		the sampled combined point on the sampled combined line; then $t$ presents
 627		the point part of the sampled point on the image line, the combining part of
 628		the sampled point is $\alpha_0 + t w$ in the diagonal case and $\alpha_0$ in
 629		the axis-parallel case, and the combined line answer evaluated at $t$ is
 630		$\sum_r (\alpha_0 + t w)_r f_r(t)$, respectively $\sum_r (\alpha_0)_r f_r(t)$;
 631		substituting the original clause $f_r(t) = b_r$ for every $r$ turns this into
 632		the combined point answer $\sum_r \alpha_r b_r$.  For a line-versus-line
 633		clause, equality of the two original tuples of line answers gives equality of
 634		their combinations.
 635	
 636		On the event $\mathbf{i} \ge m$ all measured questions are the point question
 637		at the same point $u$, and every clause of the combined game follows from the
 638		agreement $b = b'$ of the two original point answers, which is the
 639		point-versus-point clause of the original game: in cases~3 and~5 the combined
 640		line answer is a fixed affine function of the measured tuple, and evaluating
 641		it at the parameter presenting the sampled combined point returns
 642		$\sum_r \alpha_r b_r$, the combined point answer computed from the same
 643		tuple.
 644	
 645		Each branch of the combined game is therefore rejected with probability at
 646		most the sum of two branch rejection probabilities of the original game, and
 647		summing over the nine branches bounds the rejection probability of
 648		$\mathcal S^{\mathrm c}$ by an absolute multiple of that of $\mathcal S$.
 649	\end{proof}
